# GitHub commits

## When to commit

- Create commits **only when the user explicitly asks**. Do not commit opportunistically after edits.
- Do **not** push to GitHub unless the user explicitly asks.

## Commit author (default identity)

Use this author on **every** commit created in this repo. Do **not** run `git config` (global or local).

| Field | Value |
|-------|--------|
| `user.name` | `saroj990` |
| `user.email` | `saroj990@github.com` |

Pass both via `-c` on the `git commit` invocation:

```bash
git -c user.name=saroj990 -c user.email=saroj990@github.com commit -m "$(cat <<'EOF'
<type>(<scope>): subject

EOF
)"
```

If the user gives a different email for commits, use that instead and update this rule.

## Before committing

Run in parallel: `git status`, `git diff` (staged and unstaged), `git log -5 --oneline` (match existing message style when present).

Stage only relevant files. **Never** commit secrets (see `secrets-env.mdc`). Warn if the user tries to include them.

## Commit message format

Use [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/) with a short, imperative subject and optional body focused on **why**:

```text
<type>(<scope>): <subject>

<body — optional, 1–2 sentences>
```

**Types:** `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `style`, `refactor`, `test`, `chore`, `ci`, `build`.

**Scope (examples):** `frontend`, `backend`, `goals`, `tasks`, `kanban`, `dashboard`, `docker`, `deps`.

Pass the message via HEREDOC with the **commit author `-c` flags** above (not plain `git commit` without them).

## Safety

- Do not change `git config`.
- No destructive commands (`push --force`, `reset --hard`, etc.) unless the user explicitly requests them; never force-push `main`/`master`.
- No `--no-verify` / hook skips unless the user explicitly requests them.
- Prefer a **new** commit over `git commit --amend` unless amend is explicitly requested and the last commit was not pushed.

## Pull requests

Use `gh` for GitHub PRs when asked. Summarize all commits on the branch, not only the latest.
